"title='MostShittyEDR: Building a Terrible EDR to Learn Bypassing – Der Benji – Father, DevSecOps, Cyber Security enthusiast, agilist with common sense' description=' Endpoint Detection and Response tools are the closest thing the blue team has to omniscience. They sit inside every process, tap into the kernel, and watch every syscall. And yet red teamers bypass them every day. The fastest way to understand why is to build one yourself, deliberately badly, and then try to break it. That is exactly what MostShittyEDR is: an intentionally vulnerable EDR written in Nim that runs both as a user mode agent and, optionally, with a full kernel driver that provides real time process callbacks, LSASS handle protection, and hardware enforced kill.\\n\\n, blog-description' url='https://benjitrapp.github.io/MostShittyEDR-post/' language=None keywords=None robots=None og_title='MostShittyEDR: Building a Terrible EDR to Learn Bypassing' og_description=' Endpoint Detection and Response tools are the closest thing the blue team has to omniscience. They sit inside every process, tap into the kernel, and watch every syscall. And yet red teamers bypass them every day. The fastest way to understand why is to build one yourself, deliberately badly, and then try to break it. That is exactly what MostShittyEDR is: an intentionally vulnerable EDR written in Nim that runs both as a user mode agent and, optionally, with a full kernel driver that provides real time process callbacks, LSASS handle protection, and hardware enforced kill.\\n\\n' og_url=None og_image=None og_audio=None og_determiner=None og_locale=None og_locale_alternate=None og_site_name=None og_video=None favicon='https://benjitrapp.github.io/images/favicon.ico' dc_terms_created=None dc_date_created=None dc_date=None dc_terms_type=None dc_type=None dc_terms_audience=None dc_terms_subject=None dc_subject=None dc_description=None dc_terms_keywords=None modified_time=None published_time=None article_tag=None article_section=None 